      <p class="byline">Battery Basics · Life &amp; Aging · Last reviewed 2026-09-25</p>
      <h1>Heat and Battery Life: The Degradation Rule</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Everyone worries about cold cranking; almost nobody prices in heat. Yet heat is the dominant
        accelerator of battery aging — a quiet, compounding tax collected every hour the battery sits
        above its comfort temperature.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Battery aging reactions follow an Arrhenius-type temperature
        dependence — as a rule of thumb, <strong>aging speed roughly doubles for every 10°C above
        25°C</strong>. For lead-acid, heat drives grid corrosion and water loss, and can push VRLA into
        thermal runaway; for lithium, it accelerates SEI growth and electrolyte decomposition. The
        float-life derating for lead-acid is documented in the
        <a href="/lead-acid/heat-degradation/">heat degradation</a> deep page.
      </div>

      <h2>The rule of thumb and its limits</h2>
      <p>
        The "double every 10°C" rule is an approximation of the Arrhenius equation — the same physics
        that governs the <a href="/basics/self-discharge-rates-by-chemistry/">self-discharge rate</a>.
        It is a rule of thumb, not a warranty clause: real batteries have temperature windows, cooling
        design and chemistry-specific thresholds. But it is the right mental model, because it tells you
        the one fact buyers routinely ignore: <strong>a battery's life is set by its hot hours, not its
        calendar age</strong>. Two identical batteries, one in a temperate warehouse and one in a hot
        one, age on different clocks.
      </p>

      <h2>What heat does to lead-acid</h2>
      <p>
        Lead-acid chemistry dies in heat two ways. <strong>Grid corrosion</strong> — the structural lead
        of the plates corroding away — is temperature-driven and irreversible. <strong>Water loss</strong>
        accelerates too: hotter charging means more gassing, which a flooded battery survives by being
        topped up, but a sealed <a href="/lead-acid/agm-battery/">AGM</a> or gel battery cannot — every
        gram of lost water is lost life. The worst case is <strong>thermal runaway</strong> in VRLA
        designs: heat raises current, current raises heat, in a loop that ends in venting or worse. The
        mechanism and the mitigation are detailed on the technology sub-site's
        <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/vrla/">VRLA reference</a>, and the
        <a href="/lead-acid/heat-degradation/">lead-acid heat degradation</a> page covers the numbers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Float life is the practical consequence: a standby battery's design life is specified at a
        reference temperature (commonly 20–25°C), and operation above it derates the life — the
        <a href="/lead-acid/ups-battery/">UPS battery</a> and
        <a href="/lead-acid/telecom-battery/">telecom battery</a> pages show where this bites hardest,
        because standby batteries spend their whole lives charging in warm rooms.
      </p>

      <h2>What heat does to lithium</h2>
      <p>
        Lithium's hot-weather damage is subtler but just as real: elevated temperature accelerates
        <strong>SEI growth</strong> on the anode and <strong>electrolyte decomposition</strong>, both of
        which permanently consume the lithium ions that carry charge — capacity that is not lost to use,
        but to the clock. It is why <a href="/lithium/lithium-degradation/">lithium degradation</a> is
        usually reported as calendar aging plus cycle aging, with temperature multiplying the calendar
        term. And heat is the precondition for the worst case:
        <a href="/lithium/thermal-runaway/">thermal runaway</a> needs elevated temperature to sustain
        itself once triggered. A hot-climate lithium installation is not just an efficiency question —
        it is a safety design question.
      </p>

      <h2>Buying for a hot climate</h2>
      <p>
        The procurement answer mirrors the cold-climate one: <strong>spec for the climate, not the
        showroom</strong>. For lead-acid, prefer chemistries and constructions rated for the
        environment — AGM where sealed is required, flooded with a watering regime where maintenance
        exists — and understand the float-life derating before quoting a design life. For lithium,
        require the pack's stated operating and storage temperature ranges, verify the BMS thermal
        protection thresholds, and check whether the application's duty cycle actually respects them.
        The <a href="/compare/agm-vs-efb-vs-flooded-tco/">AGM vs EFB vs flooded TCO</a> comparison shows
        how climate tilts the economics, and
        <a href="/basics/battery-transport-regulations/">transport regulations</a> matter here too — a
        hot container is part of the battery's life story.
      </p>

      <h2>What procurement buyers should check</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Is the <strong>design life quoted at a stated temperature</strong> — and what does it derate to at the customer's actual operating temperature?</li>
        <li>For VRLA, is the application's ambient temperature inside the <strong>thermal-runaway safety window</strong> the manufacturer specifies?</li>
        <li>For lithium, does the datasheet state <strong>operating and storage temperature ranges</strong>, and does the BMS enforce its own thermal cut-offs?</li>
        <li>Does the <strong>warranty</strong> quietly exclude damage from temperatures the battery will realistically see — the exclusion that voids hot-climate claims?</li>
        <li>Is warehouse and container storage <strong>shaded and ventilated</strong>, with a maximum-temperature rule written into the logistics spec?</li>
        <li>Is the heat-aging expectation cross-checked against references — the <a href="https://technology.worldbatteryhub.com/vrla/">VRLA reference</a> on the technology sub-site and the <a href="/testing/cycle-life-measured/">cycle life</a> test methodology?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How much does heat shorten battery life?</h3>
      <p>As a widely used rule of thumb, battery aging reactions roughly double in speed for every 10°C above the 25°C reference — a battery that lasts five years at 25°C can fall to roughly half that in a consistently hot engine bay or warehouse. The relationship is approximate, but the direction is certain: heat is the dominant accelerator of chemical aging in both lead-acid and lithium.</p>
      <h3>What does heat do to a lead-acid battery?</h3>
      <p>Heat accelerates grid corrosion and water loss, the two classic lead-acid killers. In flooded batteries the water escapes faster; in sealed VRLA types it cannot be replaced, and sustained heat can push a VRLA battery into thermal runaway — self-heating feedback that ends in venting or worse. Every lead-acid battery's float life is specified at a reference temperature, and life derates sharply above it.</p>
      <h3>Does heat damage lithium batteries too?</h3>
      <p>Yes, through different chemistry: elevated temperature accelerates electrolyte decomposition and SEI growth on the anode, which permanently consumes the lithium that carries charge. A lithium pack stored or cycled hot ages faster than the same pack kept cool, and heat is also the precondition for thermal runaway when a fault occurs.</p>
      <h3>Is the 10°C rule exact?</h3>
      <p>No — it is an Arrhenius-based approximation with chemistry-specific windows and thresholds, not a warranty formula. It is reliable for direction and rough magnitude: hot operation shortens life in both major chemistries, and cool, ventilated storage is the cheapest life extension available.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>
        The 10°C doubling rule is presented as the standard Arrhenius-based rule of thumb, not a
        guarantee; chemistry-specific mechanisms (grid corrosion, water loss, SEI growth) follow from
        battery engineering literature and manufacturer float-life and thermal guidance.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery engineering literature — Arrhenius temperature dependence of aging reactions.</li>
        <li>Battery manufacturer documentation — float life vs temperature derating, thermal-runaway windows.</li>
        <li>Lithium cell manufacturer guidance — storage and operating temperature limits, SEI aging.</li>
        <li>Cross-references: <a href="/lead-acid/heat-degradation/">Lead-acid heat degradation</a>, <a href="/lithium/lithium-degradation/">Lithium degradation</a>, <a href="/basics/self-discharge-rates-by-chemistry/">Self-discharge by chemistry</a>, <a href="/lithium/thermal-runaway/">Thermal runaway</a>.</li>
      </ul>

